EXPRESS.JS NOTES

1. WHAT IS EXPRESS.JS?

Express.js is a fast, lightweight and flexible web application framework for Node.js.

It is used to:
- Create web servers
- Create APIs
- Handle HTTP requests and responses
- Manage routes
- Use middleware
- Build REST APIs

Definition:
Express.js is a Node.js framework used to build web servers and APIs easily.


2. INSTALL EXPRESS.JS

Create a Node.js project:

npm init -y

Install Express:

npm install express


3. BASIC EXPRESS SERVER

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server is running");
});

Explanation:

require("express")
Imports the Express module.

express()
Creates an Express application.

app.listen()
Starts the server on a particular port.


4. BASIC GET REQUEST

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Hello World");
});

app.get()
Handles a GET request.

req
Means request.

res
Means response.

res.send()
Sends a response to the client.


5. ROUTING

Routing means defining how the server responds to different URLs.

Example:

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Home Page");
});

app.get("/about", (req, res) => {
    res.send("About Page");
});

app.get("/contact", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Contact Page");
});


6. HTTP METHODS

GET
Used to read or retrieve data.

POST
Used to create or send data.

PUT
Used to update existing data.

PATCH
Used to partially update data.

DELETE
Used to delete data.


7. GET REQUEST

app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
    res.send("All Users");
});


8. POST REQUEST

app.post("/users", (req, res) => {
    res.send("User Created");
});


9. PUT REQUEST

app.put("/users/1", (req, res) => {
    res.send("User Updated");
});


10. DELETE REQUEST

app.delete("/users/1", (req, res) => {
    res.send("User Deleted");
});


11. REQUEST AND RESPONSE

Request:
Request is the data sent by the client to the server.

Request can contain:
- URL
- Parameters
- Query
- Headers
- Body

Response:
Response is the data sent by the server back to the client.


12. res.send()

Used to send a response.

Example:

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Hello Lakshy");
});


13. res.json()

Used to send JSON data.

Example:

app.get("/user", (req, res) => {
    res.json({
        id: 1,
        name: "Lakshy",
        course: "BSc CS"
    });
});


14. ROUTE PARAMETERS

Route parameters are dynamic values inside the URL.

Example:

app.get("/user/:id", (req, res) => {
    res.send(req.params.id);
});

URL:

http://localhost:3000/user/101

Output:

101

:req.params

Used to access route parameters.


15. MULTIPLE ROUTE PARAMETERS

app.get("/user/:id/:name", (req, res) => {
    res.json(req.params);
});

URL:

/user/101/Lakshy

Output:

{
    "id": "101",
    "name": "Lakshy"
}


16. QUERY PARAMETERS

Query parameters are values sent after ? in a URL.

Example:

/users?name=Lakshy&age=21

Code:

app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
    res.json(req.query);
});

Output:

{
    "name": "Lakshy",
    "age": "21"
}

req.query

Used to access query parameters.


17. ROUTE PARAMETER VS QUERY PARAMETER

Route Parameter:

/users/101

Access using:

req.params


Query Parameter:

/users?id=101

Access using:

req.query


18. REQUEST BODY

Request body contains data sent by the client to the server.

Example JSON:

{
    "name": "Lakshy",
    "age": 21
}

Access using:

req.body

To read JSON data, use:

app.use(express.json());


19. EXPRESS.JSON()

express.json() is built-in middleware.

It is used to parse incoming JSON data.

Example:

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.post("/user", (req, res) => {
    console.log(req.body);
    res.send("Data received");
});


20. MIDDLEWARE

Middleware is a function that runs during the request-response cycle.

Example:

app.use((req, res, next) => {
    console.log("Middleware executed");
    next();
});

next()

next() passes control to the next middleware or route.


21. TYPES OF MIDDLEWARE

1. Application-level middleware
2. Router-level middleware
3. Built-in middleware
4. Error-handling middleware
5. Third-party middleware


22. BUILT-IN MIDDLEWARE

Express provides built-in middleware.

Example:

app.use(express.json());

Used to parse JSON request body.

Another example:

app.use(express.static("public"));

Used to serve static files.


23. STATIC FILES

Static files are files such as:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript
Images

Example:

app.use(express.static("public"));


24. EXPRESS ROUTER

Router is used to organize routes into separate files.

Example:

const express = require("express");

const router = express.Router();

router.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Home");
});

router.get("/about", (req, res) => {
    res.send("About");
});

module.exports = router;


Use router in main file:

const router = require("./router");

app.use("/", router);


25. STATUS CODES

200 = OK
201 = Created
400 = Bad Request
401 = Unauthorized
403 = Forbidden
404 = Not Found
500 = Internal Server Error

Example:

res.status(404).send("User not found");


26. ERROR HANDLING

Express provides error-handling middleware.

Example:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    res.status(500).send("Something went wrong");
});


27. REST API

REST API is used for communication between frontend and backend.

Example:

GET       /users
POST      /users
GET       /users/101
PUT       /users/101
DELETE    /users/101


28. CRUD

CRUD means:

C = Create
R = Read
U = Update
D = Delete

CRUD and HTTP methods:

Create = POST
Read = GET
Update = PUT / PATCH
Delete = DELETE


29. EXPRESS + MONGODB

In MERN stack:

React
   ↓
Express
   ↓
Node.js
   ↓
MongoDB

Express handles API requests and Node.js communicates with MongoDB.


30. COMPLETE BASIC EXPRESS SERVER

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Home Page");
});

app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
    res.json([
        {
            id: 1,
            name: "Lakshy"
        },
        {
            id: 2,
            name: "Rahul"
        }
    ]);
});

app.post("/users", (req, res) => {
    console.log(req.body);
    res.status(201).send("User Created");
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});


31. IMPORTANT EXPRESS FUNCTIONS

express()
Creates Express application.

app.listen()
Starts the server.

app.get()
Handles GET request.

app.post()
Handles POST request.

app.put()
Handles PUT request.

app.patch()
Handles PATCH request.

app.delete()
Handles DELETE request.

app.use()
Used for middleware.

res.send()
Sends a response.

res.json()
Sends JSON response.

res.status()
Sets HTTP status code.

req.params
Accesses route parameters.

req.query
Accesses query parameters.

req.body
Accesses request body.


